# AUTHOR PARENS — an interior comment after a paren-carrying LAST ELEMENT
# stays inside the container it annotates.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture's SUBJECT is fmt
# output, so sweeping it would bake whatever the formatter does today in as the
# author's intent.
#
# GRADUATED from `known_gaps/fmt_comment_escapes_call_with_author_paren.gg`.
# The defect: the comment was emitted AFTER the call's closing paren, at
# enclosing-block indent, so it documented the next statement instead. The
# control below — the same shape with no author paren — was always correct, so
# the author paren was the discriminator.
#
# ROOT: `parse_paren_expr` returns the INNER node and pushes the grouping paren
# to a sideband, so an element's recorded span STOPS BEFORE the author's `)`.
# `paren_tuple_gate`'s closing-delimiter scan starts at that span end and so
# locked onto the AUTHOR's `)` rather than the call's, truncating the gated
# window until the comment fell outside it.
#
# ⚠ TWO COSTUMES, and the OBVIOUS fix catches neither reliably. An exact-span
# lookup in the author-paren table works only when the element's span IS the
# wrapped node's span:
#   * COSTUME 1, the element IS the parenthesised expression. In a BRACKET list
#     the two spans coincide and an exact-span lookup finds it; in a CALL they
#     do not, because a call argument is a `Spanned<CallArg>` spanned from the
#     argument's own start.
#   * COSTUME 2, the paren sits at the TAIL of a larger element. A binary node
#     is spanned `lhs.merge(paren-stripped rhs)`, so its span key differs from
#     the recorded one in BOTH containers.
# The fix advances past every layer whose span ENDS AT the element's end
# (`AuthorParenTable::layers_ending_at`), which covers both.
#
# ⚠ The damaged output was IDEMPOTENT and re-parsed cleanly, so no round-trip
# gate could ever see it. Only an explicit placement assertion can — which is
# why this fixture asserts the whole expected text.
void sink(int a, int b):
    print(a + b)

int add1(int a):
    return a

void main():
    # COSTUME 1 — the element IS the parenthesised expression
    sink(1, (2 + 3)
        # after the paren'd last arg
    )
    int y = 2
    # COSTUME 2 — the paren sits at the TAIL of a larger element
    print(add1(
        1 + (y)
        # tail of a binary whose rhs is parenthesised
    ))
    # CONTROL — same shape, no author paren. Always behaved correctly.
    sink(1, 2 + 3
        # control
    )
